# Task 4 Fix Round 1 Independent Review

## Verdict

**FAIL — two original Important findings remain open, and one new Important attestation gap was found.**

The stale-snapshot architecture and early-winner wording are fixed. Cadence, industry activation/top-N/
minimum size, and the named M settings are now represented and consumed correctly. The policy still is not
a complete effective-policy attestation, however: material hard-coded M/RS/N/S/I rules remain outside the
schema, custom strategy configuration is represented only by its Python type, and behavior-bearing state on
the owned built-in strategy is invisible to the live verifier.

## Original finding status

### ADDRESSED — Critical: one-time construction snapshot could attest stale live policy

**Confidence: 10/10.**

The fix now has the required lifecycle:

- `run()` captures a fresh live policy at the official start, before cache clearing and data access
  (`core/backtest_engine.py:1494-1524`). This replaces the constructor snapshot and therefore preserves
  supported pre-run changes to represented simulator policy such as `max_positions`.
- `_verify_effective_engine_policy` rebuilds the live object and digest, compares both with the stored object
  and digest, and independently rehashes the stored object (`core/backtest_engine.py:1478-1492`). It does not
  rely only on rehashing the old snapshot.
- Verification runs before checkpoint fingerprint construction (`core/backtest_engine.py:1194-1242`), at
  the start of every simulated session before exits or signals (`core/backtest_engine.py:1713-1725`), before
  result config (`core/backtest_engine.py:2036-2046`), before every checkpoint payload
  (`core/backtest_engine.py:2103-2121`), and before completed-checkpoint result restoration
  (`core/backtest_engine.py:2151-2159`).
- Future PIT publication calls the live simulator verifier and then requires the live digest, the config
  digest, and a canonical rehash of the config policy to agree (`pit_baseline.py:1474-1487`). Only after this
  check are the summary and manifest policy variables used (`pit_baseline.py:1488-1508`, `1529-1546`). They
  are defined on every successful publication path.

The implementer's accepted direct probes also demonstrate a supported pre-run mutation being recaptured and
a live `settings.SCALE_OUT_TIERS` mutation being rejected (`task-4-report.md:121-143`). I did not rerun them,
as required.

The new nested-strategy coverage issue below limits the completeness of the resulting attestation, but it is
not the original stale-object/stale-digest implementation defect: for every field that the policy actually
represents, live rebuilding and boundary verification are now correctly implemented.

### OPEN — Important: active cadence, industry, M, and broader scoring behavior was outside the policy

**Confidence: 10/10.**

The specifically reported cadence and industry omissions are addressed. The policy now contains classified
leaves for cadence, industry activation/mode/top-N/minimum size (`core/engine_policy.py:230-263`); execution
uses cadence at `core/backtest_engine.py:1748-1755` and passes the captured industry top-N/minimum size
explicitly into ranking before applying the attested activation flag (`core/backtest_engine.py:2352-2373`).
All settings-backed built-in M evaluator and stateful-regime inputs named in the first review are also present
at `core/engine_policy.py:399-470`, and their live consumers read those same settings in
`core/canslim/m_market_direction.py:92-116`, `140-185`, and `354-368`, `401-443`.

The finding remains open because the new claim of a complete behavior-bearing surface is broader than those
settings, and material fixed inputs are still omitted from the only returned M/scoring mappings:

- Built-in M follow-through detection consumes a default 30-session search window and a hard-coded `-0.01`
  rally-reset threshold (`core/canslim/m_market_direction.py:252-258`, `282-314`); `evaluate_m` calls it
  without overriding the 30-session window (`core/canslim/m_market_direction.py:412-418`). It also has an
  active `<50`-row fallback with score `0.4` and bearish state (`core/canslim/m_market_direction.py:378-385`).
  None appears in the complete market mapping at `core/engine_policy.py:399-470`.
- The RS snapshot requires at least ten ranked performances before returning any scores
  (`core/momentum_analysis.py:126-146`). The policy records quarterly settings, the 252-day fallback, and
  60-session per-symbol history, but not that ten-symbol gate (`core/engine_policy.py:298-324`).
- N scoring materially uses proximity bands `0.98`, `0.90`, and `0.75` plus the lower-band `0.3` factor
  (`core/canslim/n_new_products.py:125-159`). S scoring uses fixed power-gap thresholds `0.02` and `1.5`, a
  50-session volume baseline/lookback, fixed up/down-volume caps, and float bands
  (`core/canslim/s_supply_demand.py:77-140`, `143-216`, `281-321`). I scoring uses fixed ownership and holder-
  trend bands (`core/canslim/i_institutional.py:24-96`). The policy's complete N/S/I inventory contains only
  settings-backed weights/targets and the explicit S proximity value (`core/engine_policy.py:360-395`).

These are strategy decisions, not causal invariants or run data. They can be represented as exact fixed
threshold leaves or truthful sourced formula leaves without inspecting prices, trades, caches, or artifacts.
Until they are, the digest cannot truthfully stand for the complete built-in scoring/M policy asserted by
`task-4-report.md:84-90`, `170-172`.

Required fix: finish the behavior inventory for the built-in M/RS/N/S/I paths and wrap the material fixed
thresholds/formulas with sources and classifications. Supported policy thresholds/weights remain later
optimizer candidates; causal timing and data-visibility invariants remain non-candidates.

### OPEN — Important: injected-strategy compatibility and policy claims

**Confidence: 9/10.**

The original compatibility regression is fixed: `min_c_a_growth` is validated only for the owned built-in
strategy (`core/backtest_engine.py:1439-1467`), and built-in-only scoring and unsupported leaves are omitted
for injected strategies (`core/engine_policy.py:325-326`, `634-640`). A minimal custom strategy therefore no
longer has to invent a `CanslimStrategy` attribute.

The identity/claim portion remains open. The custom policy identity is only
`{type(strategy).__module__, type(strategy).__qualname__}` (`core/engine_policy.py:273-289`), after which the
custom mapping returns without describing its scoring configuration (`core/engine_policy.py:298-326`). Two
instances of the same class with different thresholds, rulebooks, or modes therefore have the same effective
policy and digest. This is not hypothetical in the supported codebase: `CachedDiagnosisStrategy` behavior
depends on its `rulebook`, `experiment`, and `strict_canslim` constructor state
(`core/pit_diagnosis/strategy.py:158-190`), yet all such instances share one type identity. The engine already
has a JSON-safe explicit `checkpoint_identity` contract for custom strategies
(`core/backtest_engine.py:1075-1132`), but it is not part of the effective policy and is evaluated only for
checkpoint identity.

Required fix: do not imply that type provenance attests custom policy configuration. Bind a declared stable,
JSON-safe custom policy identity where one exists, or explicitly classify custom strategy internals as
opaque/unattested and scope the digest claim to the engine-owned canonical envelope. The latter preserves
custom-strategy compatibility without inspecting mutable runtime balances, trades, prices, or artifacts.

### ADDRESSED — Important: early-winner leaf overstated the hold

**Confidence: 10/10.**

The leaf now says that a 20% gain by held day 15 suppresses only tiered scale-outs while the flag is active,
that the flag clears at the start of processing when `days_held >= 40`, and that hard stop, stagnation,
protective-stop, and EMA behavior remains active (`core/engine_policy.py:566-581`). That exactly matches flag
release/trigger ordering, the unconditional hard stop, the guarded scale-out block, and the later exit logic
at `core/backtest_engine.py:2672-2720`. No exit behavior was changed.

## New findings

### Important — Live verification does not cover behavior-bearing state on the owned built-in strategy

**Confidence: 9/10.**

The policy records simulator-level `technical_only`/proper-base/market fields and the strategy's type, but it
does not record or compare the owned strategy's duplicated execution inputs (`core/engine_policy.py:224-289`).
Actual built-in execution branches on `strategy.require_proper_base` and `strategy.technical_only`, and uses
`strategy.fundamental_provider` (`core/backtest_engine.py:743-824`, `878-900`). Those attributes are assigned
at construction (`core/backtest_engine.py:621-672`, `1381-1412`) but are not frozen.

Consequently, changing one of those nested behavior inputs after capture can change scoring/data selection
while `_verify_effective_engine_policy` rebuilds the same policy and passes. A pre-run change to the
simulator-level mode can also be recaptured without synchronizing its owned strategy copy, producing an
attested simulator mode that is not the mode used for built-in score construction. This is the same class of
hidden mutable policy the live verifier is intended to eliminate, although it is distinct from the original
stored-object bug.

Required fix: make the owned strategy consume the attested engine-owned values, or add its material bound
values/provider mode to the live policy and fail closed on disagreement. Do not include provider objects or
runtime data themselves; a stable mode/provenance marker is sufficient.

No new Critical or Minor findings were identified.

## Other verified requirements

- Constructor-time inert validation still precedes `DataFetcher` construction
  (`core/backtest_engine.py:1323-1351`, `1380`), and run-start revalidation precedes cache/data access
  (`core/backtest_engine.py:1512-1524`). Non-default inert requests therefore fail closed at the required
  boundary.
- Canonical hashing remains sorted, compact UTF-8 JSON with `allow_nan=False`
  (`core/engine_policy.py:61-67`, `664-667`). The digest exists after construction and run-start capture,
  is available to checkpoint fingerprint creation, and is present in result config
  (`core/backtest_engine.py:1437`, `1512`, `1194-1242`, `2097-2098`).
- The actual entry thresholds, component/C/A weights, metric priority, non-M composite, M settings, capacity
  and eviction, risk-sizing formula and inputs, scale tiers, and configured exits that are already represented
  retain the values/classifications verified in the first review (`core/engine_policy.py:169-264`, `327-395`,
  `399-589`). CANSLIM remains the declared feature basis and optimization remains future work
  (`core/engine_policy.py:644-659`); causal invariants remain optimizer non-candidates.
- No normal-default strategy semantic change was found in the fix diff. In particular, the industry change
  preserves the existing provider/non-technical activation condition while making the already-bound defaults
  explicit at execution (`core/backtest_engine.py:1415-1422`, `1654-1659`, `2357-2373`). The additional
  failures are policy-attestation/inert-request fail-closed paths, not optimizer tuning.
- `core.engine_policy` does not import `core.backtest_engine`, so no circular import was introduced
  (`core/engine_policy.py:1-20`). The scoped package changes only the authorized production files and report;
  no sealed Task 11 artifact was rewritten.
- Requiring `_verify_effective_engine_policy` and authenticated policy keys at PIT publication
  (`pit_baseline.py:1474-1487`) intentionally rejects the old `FakePortfolio`, which exposes neither
  (`tests/test_pit_baseline.py:77-115`). This is not classified as a production defect: Task 4 explicitly
  strengthens the future output contract, and accepting or synthesizing an unauthenticated fake policy would
  defeat it. The fixture should be brought forward when test edits are permitted. `_run_portfolio`'s existing
  test-double accommodation concerns resumability-call compatibility (`pit_baseline.py:1208-1239`), not a
  waiver of publication integrity.

## Review constraints observed

The refreshed report's direct probes, compilation, and diff-check evidence were accepted without rerunning.
No test, backtest, provider/network command, cache refresh, optimizer operation, download, or Git mutation was
performed. Production sources were inspected read-only; only this review artifact was created.
